content:
To move existing top-level resources under a new `ComponentResource`, each child must alias its former **root-level URN**. Keep the child’s Pulumi logical name and resource type unchanged.

## Before: flat program

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";

const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const dbConnectionString = cfg.requireSecret("dbConnectionString");

const assets = new aws.s3.Bucket("assets", {
    forceDestroy: false,
});

const assetsVersioning = new aws.s3.BucketVersioningV2("assets-versioning", {
    bucket: assets.id,
    versioningConfiguration: {
        status: "Enabled",
    },
});

const assetsEncryption = new aws.s3.BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2(
    "assets-encryption",
    {
        bucket: assets.id,
        rules: [{
            applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: {
                sseAlgorithm: "AES256",
            },
        }],
    },
);

const assetsPublicAccess = new aws.s3.BucketPublicAccessBlock("assets-public-access", {
    bucket: assets.id,
    blockPublicAcls: true,
    blockPublicPolicy: true,
    ignorePublicAcls: true,
    restrictPublicBuckets: true,
});

const database = new aws.rds.Instance("database", {
    engine: "postgres",
    instanceClass: "db.t3.micro",
    allocatedStorage: 20,
    backupRetentionPeriod: 7,
    deletionProtection: true,
    publiclyAccessible: false,
    multiAz: false,
    storageEncrypted: true,
    username: "app",
    password: dbConnectionString,
    skipFinalSnapshot: false,
    finalSnapshotIdentifier: "database-final",
});
```

Their old URNs are conceptually:

```text
urn:pulumi:dev::project::aws:s3/bucket:Bucket::assets
urn:pulumi:dev::project::aws:s3/bucketVersioningV2:BucketVersioningV2::assets-versioning
urn:pulumi:dev::project::aws:rds/instance:Instance::database
```

They have no custom parent beyond the stack.

---

## After: component with root-parent aliases

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import * as aws from "@pulumi/aws";

export interface WebPlatformArgs {
    dbConnectionString: pulumi.Input<string>;

    dbInstanceClass: pulumi.Input<string>;
    dbMultiAz: pulumi.Input<boolean>;

    // Other environment-specific inputs:
    nodeInstanceType: pulumi.Input<string>;
    desiredNodeCount: pulumi.Input<number>;
    production: pulumi.Input<boolean>;
}

export class WebPlatform extends pulumi.ComponentResource {
    public readonly assetsBucket: aws.s3.Bucket;
    public readonly database: aws.rds.Instance;

    constructor(
        name: string,
        args: WebPlatformArgs,
        opts?: pulumi.ComponentResourceOptions,
    ) {
        super("example:platform:WebPlatform", name, {}, opts);

        /*
         * Each resource is now parented by this component, but its alias says
         * that the same resource previously existed directly under the stack.
         *
         * mergeOptions preserves provider, dependsOn, protect, and other
         * resource-specific options supplied through `extra`.
         */
        const adopted = (
            extra?: pulumi.CustomResourceOptions,
        ): pulumi.CustomResourceOptions =>
            pulumi.mergeOptions(extra, {
                parent: this,
                aliases: [{
                    parent: pulumi.rootStackResource,
                }],
            });

        this.assetsBucket = new aws.s3.Bucket("assets", {
            forceDestroy: false,
        }, adopted({
            protect: true,
        }));

        const assetsVersioning =
            new aws.s3.BucketVersioningV2("assets-versioning", {
                bucket: this.assetsBucket.id,
                versioningConfiguration: {
                    status: "Enabled",
                },
            }, adopted());

        const assetsEncryption =
            new aws.s3.BucketServerSideEncryptionConfigurationV2(
                "assets-encryption",
                {
                    bucket: this.assetsBucket.id,
                    rules: [{
                        applyServerSideEncryptionByDefault: {
                            sseAlgorithm: "AES256",
                        },
                    }],
                },
                adopted(),
            );

        const assetsPublicAccess =
            new aws.s3.BucketPublicAccessBlock("assets-public-access", {
                bucket: this.assetsBucket.id,
                blockPublicAcls: true,
                blockPublicPolicy: true,
                ignorePublicAcls: true,
                restrictPublicBuckets: true,
            }, adopted());

        this.database = new aws.rds.Instance("database", {
            engine: "postgres",
            instanceClass: args.dbInstanceClass,
            allocatedStorage: 20,

            backupRetentionPeriod: 7,
            deletionProtection: true,
            publiclyAccessible: false,

            multiAz: args.dbMultiAz,
            storageEncrypted: true,

            username: "app",
            password: args.dbConnectionString,

            skipFinalSnapshot: false,
            finalSnapshotIdentifier: "database-final",
        }, adopted({
            protect: true,
        }));

        this.registerOutputs({
            assetsBucketName: this.assetsBucket.bucket,
            databaseEndpoint: this.database.endpoint,
        });
    }
}
```

Instantiate it from the stack entry point:

```ts
import * as pulumi from "@pulumi/pulumi";
import { WebPlatform } from "./webPlatform";

const cfg = new pulumi.Config();
const environment = pulumi.getStack();

const production = environment === "prod";

const platform = new WebPlatform("web", {
    production,

    nodeInstanceType: production ? "t3.large" : "t3.medium",
    desiredNodeCount: production ? 4 : 2,

    dbInstanceClass: production ? "db.t3.medium" : "db.t3.micro",
    dbMultiAz: production,

    dbConnectionString: cfg.requireSecret("dbConnectionString"),
});

export const assetsBucketName = platform.assetsBucket.bucket;
export const databaseEndpoint = platform.database.endpoint;
```

The expected first preview after migration should contain:

```text
+  example:platform:WebPlatform web create
~  aws:s3/bucket:Bucket assets update/rename URN
~  aws:rds/instance:Instance database update/rename URN
```

The exact CLI wording can vary by Pulumi version, but existing cloud resources must not show `replace`, `delete`, or `create`.

## Apply the alias to every formerly flat resource

Every resource that was previously declared at stack scope needs the root-parent alias, including supporting resources:

- EKS cluster
- managed node groups
- VPC/network resources, if they were flat
- S3 bucket
- bucket versioning
- bucket encryption
- public-access block
- replication role/policy and destination bucket
- RDS instance, subnet group, parameter/security groups
- IAM users, roles, policies, and attachments
- ACM certificate and validation resources
- ALB/listener or CloudFront distribution
- secret-storage resources, if any

Changing only the bucket’s parent does not automatically adopt separately declared versioning or encryption resources.

## If a child is renamed at the same time

Prefer migrating the parent first without renaming. If necessary, include both the old name and old parent:

```ts
const bucket = new aws.s3.Bucket("application-assets", args, {
    parent: this,
    aliases: [{
        name: "assets",
        parent: pulumi.rootStackResource,
    }],
});
```

If the Pulumi resource type also changed, specify the old type:

```ts
aliases: [{
    name: "old-name",
    type: "old-package:index:OldType",
    parent: pulumi.rootStackResource,
}]
```

Aliases preserve Pulumi identity, but they cannot make two genuinely different provider resource types safely interchangeable.

## Stack-secret configuration

Use independently deployable Pulumi stacks:

```yaml
# Pulumi.dev.yaml
config:
  aws:region: us-east-1
  project:dbConnectionString:
    secure: <Pulumi-encrypted-ciphertext>
```

```yaml
# Pulumi.prod.yaml
config:
  aws:region: us-east-1
  project:dbConnectionString:
    secure: <Pulumi-encrypted-ciphertext>
```

Set secrets without placing plaintext in Git:

```bash
pulumi stack select dev
pulumi config set --secret dbConnectionString \
  'postgresql://app:password@dev-db:5432/app'

pulumi stack select prod
pulumi config set --secret dbConnectionString \
  'postgresql://app:password@prod-db:5432/app'
```

## Safe migration procedure

1. Start from a clean deployment with no pending changes.
2. Add the component while preserving every child’s logical name, type, provider, and arguments.
3. Add `{ parent: pulumi.rootStackResource }` aliases to all formerly root-level resources.
4. Run:

   ```bash
   pulumi preview --diff
   ```

5. Confirm the component is the only newly created logical resource and no cloud resources are replaced.
6. Apply dev first:

   ```bash
   pulumi up
   ```

7. Repeat the preview and update independently for prod.

Do not alias the new component to one of the old resources. The component is genuinely new; only its children are adopting the old flat-resource identities. Also note that aliases solve URN/parent changes only—simultaneously changing immutable properties, providers, regions, or physical names can still cause replacement.
